/*!
    \qmlmodule Clayground.Sound
    \title Clayground Sound Module
    \brief Audio playback for games - sound effects and background music.

    The Clayground.Sound module provides cross-platform audio for games:
    \list
    \li \l Sound - One-shot / overlapping sound effects (pre-authored WAV)
    \li \l Music - Background music (play/pause/stop/loop/seek)
    \li \l SynthInstrument - Real-time oscillator voice with ADSR/pitch env/LFO and a resonant filter
    \li \l SampleInstrument - PCM sample playback with loop points and root note
    \li \l SongPlayer - Plays a \c .song.json pattern file against instruments
    \li \l SfxPlayer - Plays a \c .sfx.json effect recipe, with variation, live or rendered offline
    \li \l SoundMixer - The volume, echo and reverb of each bus: sound effects, music, voices
    \endlist

    \section1 Stereo, pan and buses

    The engine mixes in stereo. Every instrument has a \c pan, from -1 (left)
    to +1 (right). The centre, 0 and the default, plays the same samples in
    both channels that the engine played in mono before; panning only turns
    the far channel down, to silence at either edge.

    Every instrument also names the \c bus its voices mix into: \c "sfx"
    (the default), \c "music" or \c "voice". \l SoundMixer holds one volume
    per bus, which scales everything on that bus on top of each instrument's
    own volume, so a game's settings screen sets three numbers instead of
    touching every instrument. \l {SongPlayer::volume}{SongPlayer.volume}
    scales every note a song plays, whatever instrument plays it.

    \section1 Platform Support

    \table
    \header
        \li Platform
        \li Backend
    \row
        \li WASM (Browser)
        \li Web Audio API via Emscripten
    \row
        \li Desktop (Windows/macOS/Linux)
        \li Qt Multimedia (QSoundEffect, QMediaPlayer)
    \row
        \li Mobile (iOS/Android)
        \li Qt Multimedia
    \endtable

    \section1 Usage

    Import the module:
    \qml
    import Clayground.Sound
    \endqml

    \section2 Sound Effects

    Use \l Sound for short audio like jumps, clicks, or explosions:
    \qml
    Sound {
        id: jumpSound
        source: "sounds/jump.wav"
        volume: 0.8
    }

    // Play sound (can overlap multiple times)
    onPlayerJumped: jumpSound.play()
    \endqml

    \section2 Background Music

    Use \l Music for longer tracks with playback controls:
    \qml
    Music {
        id: bgMusic
        source: "music/theme.mp3"
        volume: 0.5
        loop: true
    }

    onGameStarted: bgMusic.play()
    onGamePaused: bgMusic.pause()
    onGameOver: bgMusic.stop()
    \endqml

    \section1 Lazy Loading

    By default, audio is preloaded when \c source is set. For memory optimization,
    enable lazy loading to defer loading until first play:
    \qml
    Sound {
        source: "sounds/rare_event.wav"
        lazyLoading: true  // Only loads when play() is called
    }
    \endqml

    \section1 Technical Notes

    \list
    \li Audio files are fully preloaded before playback (no streaming)
    \li WASM: Browser autoplay policies require user interaction before audio plays
    \li WASM: Remote URLs must have CORS headers enabled
    \li WASM: Music plays through an HTML audio element, which reports no
        duration or position back and ignores \c loop; use the \c finished
        signal or a Sound re-triggered by a Timer for a looping background
        track
    \li WASM: use \c .mp3 for Music - a staged \c .wav is labelled
        \c audio/vnd.wave, a type browsers decline, and stays silent
    \li Desktop: Uses low-latency QSoundEffect for Sound, full-featured QMediaPlayer for Music
    \endlist

    \sa Sound, Music
*/

/*!
    \qmltype Sound
    \inqmlmodule Clayground.Sound
    \inherits ClaySound
    \brief Play sound effects with support for overlapping playback.

    Sound provides an easy-to-use interface for playing short sound effects
    like jump sounds, clicks, explosions, etc. Multiple instances of the same
    sound can play simultaneously (overlapping).

    \section1 Properties

    \qmlproperty url Sound::source
    The audio file URL to load (local or remote).

    \qmlproperty real Sound::volume
    Volume level from 0.0 (silent) to 1.0 (full). Default: 1.0

    \qmlproperty real Sound::pan
    \qmlproperty string Sound::bus
    Stereo position and mixer bus, as for \l {SampleInstrument::pan}
    {SampleInstrument}.

    \qmlproperty bool Sound::lazyLoading
    If true, audio loads on first play() call. If false (default), loads immediately.

    \qmlproperty bool Sound::loaded
    Read-only. True when audio data is fully loaded and ready to play.

    \qmlproperty enumeration Sound::status
    Read-only. Current loading status:
    \list
    \li Sound.Null - No source set
    \li Sound.Loading - Currently loading
    \li Sound.Ready - Loaded and ready
    \li Sound.Error - Load failed
    \endlist

    \section1 Methods

    \qmlmethod void Sound::play()
    Plays the sound. Can be called multiple times for overlapping playback.
    If not loaded, triggers lazy loading then plays.

    \qmlmethod void Sound::stop()
    Stops all currently playing instances of this sound.

    \qmlmethod void Sound::load()
    Explicitly preloads the audio (useful with lazyLoading enabled).

    \section1 Signals

    \qmlsignal Sound::finished()
    Emitted when playback completes.

    \qmlsignal Sound::errorOccurred(string message)
    Emitted when loading or playback fails.

    \sa Music
*/

/*!
    \qmltype Music
    \inqmlmodule Clayground.Sound
    \inherits ClayMusic
    \brief Play background music with play/pause/stop/loop controls.

    Music provides an interface for playing longer audio tracks like
    background music. Supports play, pause, stop, seek, and looping.

    \section1 Properties

    \qmlproperty url Music::source
    The audio file URL to load (local or remote).

    \qmlproperty real Music::volume
    Volume level from 0.0 (silent) to 1.0 (full). Default: 1.0. The track
    plays at this volume times \l {SoundMixer::musicVolume}
    {SoundMixer.musicVolume}; the property itself keeps the value set.

    \qmlproperty bool Music::lazyLoading
    If true, audio loads on first play() call. If false (default), loads immediately.

    \qmlproperty bool Music::loaded
    Read-only. True when audio data is fully loaded and ready to play.

    \qmlproperty bool Music::playing
    Read-only. True when audio is currently playing.

    \qmlproperty bool Music::paused
    Read-only. True when audio is paused.

    \qmlproperty bool Music::loop
    If true, audio loops continuously. Default: false

    \qmlproperty int Music::position
    Read-only. Current playback position in milliseconds.

    \qmlproperty int Music::duration
    Read-only. Total duration in milliseconds.

    \qmlproperty enumeration Music::status
    Read-only. Current loading status:
    \list
    \li Music.Null - No source set
    \li Music.Loading - Currently loading
    \li Music.Ready - Loaded and ready
    \li Music.Error - Load failed
    \endlist

    \section1 Methods

    \qmlmethod void Music::play()
    Starts or resumes playback. If paused, resumes from paused position.
    If not loaded, triggers lazy loading then plays.

    \qmlmethod void Music::pause()
    Pauses playback. Position is preserved for later resume.

    \qmlmethod void Music::stop()
    Stops playback and resets position to beginning.

    \qmlmethod void Music::seek(int ms)
    Seeks to the specified position in milliseconds.

    \qmlmethod void Music::load()
    Explicitly preloads the audio (useful with lazyLoading enabled).

    \section1 Signals

    \qmlsignal Music::finished()
    Emitted when playback completes (not emitted when looping).

    \qmlsignal Music::errorOccurred(string message)
    Emitted when loading or playback fails.

    \sa Sound
*/

/*!
    \qmltype SynthInstrument
    \inqmlmodule Clayground.Sound
    \brief Real-time oscillator voice with ADSR, pitch envelope, LFO and a resonant filter.

    SynthInstrument owns an internal audio engine and one oscillator instrument.
    Call \l triggerNote() to fire a note; tweak the patch via properties. It
    can also \l bake() a note to a WAV on disk for later reuse as a
    \l SampleInstrument source.

    \section1 Properties

    \qmlproperty string SynthInstrument::waveform
    One of \c "sine", \c "square", \c "triangle", \c "sawtooth", \c "noise".

    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::pulseWidth
    For \c "square" only: the fraction of each period the wave is high,
    0.05..0.95. The default 0.5 is the plain square; 0.25 or 0.125 give the
    thinner, nasal pulses of chip sound.

    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::attack
    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::decay
    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::sustain
    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::release
    ADSR envelope, all in seconds.

    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::pitchStart
    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::pitchEnd
    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::pitchTime
    Pitch envelope: slide from \c pitchStart semitones above the trigger
    frequency to \c pitchEnd over \c pitchTime seconds. Set all to 0 for
    a flat pitch.

    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::lfoRate
    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::lfoDepth
    \qmlproperty string SynthInstrument::lfoTarget
    LFO in Hz/semitones targeting \c "pitch", \c "volume", or \c "none".

    \qmlproperty string SynthInstrument::filterType
    The filter after the oscillator: \c "none" (the default, bypassed),
    \c "lowpass", \c "highpass" or \c "bandpass". It is a two-pole
    state-variable filter, 12 dB per octave; the band-pass passes the cutoff
    at unity gain.

    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::cutoff
    Cutoff frequency in Hz, 20..20000, default 1000. Ignored while
    \l filterType is \c "none".

    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::resonance
    Peak at the cutoff, 0..1: 0 (the default) is flat (Q 0.707), 1 is Q 10,
    about +20 dB.

    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::filterAttack
    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::filterDecay
    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::filterSustain
    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::filterRelease
    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::filterEnvAmount
    Cutoff envelope: an ADSR of its own (seconds; sustain 0..1, default 1)
    that moves \l cutoff up by \c filterEnvAmount octaves at its peak, or
    down if the amount is negative. The default amount 0 keeps the cutoff
    fixed. A pluck: \c "lowpass", \c cutoff 400, \c filterEnvAmount 3,
    \c filterDecay 0.2, \c filterSustain 0.

    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::volume
    Post-mix volume, 0..1.

    \qmlproperty real SynthInstrument::pan
    Stereo position, -1 (left) .. +1 (right). The default 0 plays centred,
    the same samples in both channels. Panning turns the far channel down:
    at 0.5 the left plays at half level, the right at full. Moving it moves
    notes already ringing.

    \qmlproperty string SynthInstrument::bus
    The mixer bus the voices play through: \c "sfx" (the default),
    \c "music" or \c "voice". Its volume is set on \l SoundMixer. Any
    other name is refused with a warning and the bus stays as it was.

    \qmlproperty int SynthInstrument::maxVoices
    At most this many voices ring at once. A note that would exceed it cuts
    the oldest voice of this instrument, without a release. The default 0
    is no cap.

    \qmlproperty int SynthInstrument::activeVoices
    Read-only. Number of voices currently rendering.

    \section1 Methods

    \qmlmethod bool SynthInstrument::triggerNote(int midiNote, real velocity, real durationSeconds)
    Fire a note. \c midiNote \c 69 is A4. Returns true on success.

    \qmlmethod bool SynthInstrument::trigger(real freqHz, real velocity, real durationSeconds)
    Same as \l triggerNote() but specifies pitch in Hz.

    \qmlmethod string SynthInstrument::bake(int midiNote, real durationSeconds, real velocity)
    Render one note with the current patch into a WAV under the app cache
    directory and return its absolute path. Repeated bakes of the same
    inputs hit the cache and return the same filename.

    \qmlmethod var SynthInstrument::renderOffline(real durationSeconds)
    Returns a \c QVector<float> of audio samples (destructive: advances the
    live engine). Primarily for tests.

    \sa SampleInstrument, SongPlayer
*/

/*!
    \qmltype SampleInstrument
    \inqmlmodule Clayground.Sound
    \brief PCM sample playback with loop points, root note, and ADSR.

    Loads a WAV from \l source and lets you play it at any pitch, with
    optional loop region, shaped by an ADSR envelope on top of the sample.

    \section1 Properties

    \qmlproperty url SampleInstrument::source
    Path to a WAV file. Reloaded on change.

    \qmlproperty int SampleInstrument::rootNote
    MIDI note number that plays the sample at its native rate. Default: 60 (C4).

    \qmlproperty bool SampleInstrument::looping
    \qmlproperty real SampleInstrument::loopStart
    \qmlproperty real SampleInstrument::loopEnd
    Looping configuration. \c loopStart / \c loopEnd are fractions in 0..1
    of the sample length.

    \qmlproperty real SampleInstrument::attack
    \qmlproperty real SampleInstrument::release
    ADSR (attack/release only for now) applied on top of sample playback.

    \qmlproperty real SampleInstrument::volume
    Post-mix volume, 0..1.

    \qmlproperty real SampleInstrument::pan
    Stereo position, -1 (left) .. +1 (right); 0, the default, is centred.
    The same law as \l {SynthInstrument::pan}{SynthInstrument.pan}.

    \qmlproperty string SampleInstrument::bus
    The mixer bus: \c "sfx" (the default), \c "music" or \c "voice".

    \qmlproperty string SampleInstrument::interpolation
    How a note pitched away from \l rootNote reads between two source
    samples: \c "nearest" (the closest sample: hard and aliased, 8-bit
    grit), \c "linear" (the default, as before the modes existed) or
    \c "gaussian" (four samples weighted by a Gaussian, like the SNES:
    soft, the highs rolled off - a tone at a quarter of the sample rate
    plays at 0.63 of its level). Applies to notes triggered after the
    change. Any other name is refused with a warning.

    \qmlproperty int SampleInstrument::maxVoices
    At most this many voices ring at once; a note beyond it cuts the
    oldest. The default 0 is no cap. As for
    \l {SynthInstrument::maxVoices}{SynthInstrument.maxVoices}.

    \qmlproperty bool SampleInstrument::loaded
    \qmlproperty int  SampleInstrument::activeVoices
    \qmlproperty string SampleInstrument::errorString

    \section1 Methods

    \qmlmethod bool SampleInstrument::trigger(real freqHz, real velocity, real durationSeconds)
    \qmlmethod bool SampleInstrument::triggerNote(int midiNote, real velocity, real durationSeconds)
    \qmlmethod bool SampleInstrument::triggerOneShot(real velocity)
    \qmlmethod void SampleInstrument::stopAll()

    \sa SynthInstrument, SongPlayer
*/

/*!
    \qmltype SongPlayer
    \inqmlmodule Clayground.Sound
    \brief Plays a parsed \c .song.json file against a set of instruments.

    Resolves each track's \c instrument field against the \c objectName of
    the provided \l instruments, then advances playback position in beats
    on an internal timer, firing \c triggerNote() on the matching
    instrument. Watches the source file for changes and \e{hot-reloads}
    without resetting the playhead.

    A version 2 song file defines its own instruments and the structure
    adaptive music needs: segments with exit cues, layers an intensity
    fades in, transitions synced to beat, bar, exit or end, and stingers.
    It plays on those instruments (\l instruments is not read) and
    follows \l state, \l intensity and \l playStinger(). Its notes are
    scheduled 0.25 s ahead of the engine on their exact frames, the path
    \l renderOffline() takes, so live and offline render the same
    samples; a control reaches the music that much later.

    \qml
    SongPlayer {
        source: "songs/adaptive.song.json"
        state: game.fighting ? "combat" : "explore"
        intensity: game.danger
    }
    \endqml

    See the \l {Clayground.Sound Module} overview or the plugin README
    for the JSON format.

    \section1 Properties

    \qmlproperty url SongPlayer::source
    Path to a \c .song.json file.

    \qmlproperty var SongPlayer::instruments
    List of instrument objects (\l SynthInstrument / \l SampleInstrument
    etc). Each is looked up by its \c objectName to match track bindings.

    \qmlproperty bool SongPlayer::loaded
    True once the song parsed successfully.

    \qmlproperty bool SongPlayer::playing
    Read-only. True while actively advancing.

    \qmlproperty real SongPlayer::position
    Current position in beats; for a v2 song, beats into \l segment.

    \qmlproperty real SongPlayer::tempo
    Tempo in BPM, taken from the song file.

    \qmlproperty real SongPlayer::totalBeats
    Total length in beats (sum of section lengths \(\times\) repeats);
    for a v2 song, the length of \l segment.

    \qmlproperty bool SongPlayer::loop
    When true, playback wraps to 0 at end instead of stopping.

    \qmlproperty real SongPlayer::volume
    Volume of the whole song, 0..1, default 1. It scales every note the
    player triggers, on any of its instruments, on top of the instrument's
    own volume and its bus volume, and it keeps scaling notes while they
    ring, so animating it fades the song smoothly. Two players sharing
    instruments keep separate volumes. A QML object in \l instruments
    that forwards \c triggerNote() to an instrument passes the volume on;
    an instrument that does not play on the Clayground.Sound engine does
    not get it.

    \qmlproperty string SongPlayer::error
    Last parse/IO error, empty when OK.

    \qmlproperty int SongPlayer::version
    Read-only. The file version of the loaded song, 1 or 2.

    \qmlproperty string SongPlayer::state
    Version 2: the segment the music should play. Changing it moves the
    music there on the sync point of the first transition in the file
    that matches, or on the next bar. Set before \l play(), the segment
    the song starts in.

    \qmlproperty real SongPlayer::intensity
    Version 2: 0..1, default 0. Each layer of the song sounds from its
    low to its high intensity, its level moving over the layer's fade;
    a note plays at the level its start falls on.

    \qmlproperty string SongPlayer::segment
    Read-only. Version 2: the segment playing now.

    \section1 Methods

    \qmlmethod void SongPlayer::play()
    \qmlmethod void SongPlayer::pause()
    \qmlmethod void SongPlayer::stop()
    \qmlmethod void SongPlayer::seek(real beats)

    \qmlmethod bool SongPlayer::playStinger(string name)
    Version 2: plays the stinger \a name on top of the music, on its
    sync point. Returns false for an unknown stinger or while nothing
    plays.

    \qmlmethod list<real> SongPlayer::renderOffline(real seconds, list cues)
    \a seconds of the song from its start (0 or less: its length,
    \l totalBeats at \l tempo) as mono samples (the (L + R) / 2 downmix,
    clamped to -1..1) at 44.1 kHz, with the bound instruments' current
    patches and the player's \l volume and \l loop. Every note starts on
    its exact frame, where live playback starts it on the tick it falls
    into. Renders on an engine of its own and does not touch the live
    sound or the playhead. Only SynthInstrument and SampleInstrument
    play here; the bus volume, echo and reverb are not part of it. A C++
    program without QML renders the same samples through \c renderSong()
    of \c Clayground::SoundEngine.

    A version 2 song renders on its own instruments, from the player's
    \l state and \l intensity (0 seconds: one pass of that segment),
    and \a cues steer it as a game would:
    \c{[{ at: 2, state: "combat" }, { at: 3, intensity: 1 }, { at: 4, stinger: "pickup" }]},
    \c at in seconds. Played live, a control reaches the song 0.25 s
    after it is set; cues at those song times render what was heard.

    \section1 Signals

    \qmlsignal SongPlayer::finished()
    Emitted when the song reaches its end (not when looping).

    \qmlsignal SongPlayer::hotReloaded()
    Emitted after the source file changed on disk and was re-parsed
    successfully; playback continues from the current position.

    \qmlsignal SongPlayer::parseError(string message)
    Emitted when the source file fails to parse. On a mid-playback hot
    reload, the previous model is preserved and playback keeps going.

    \sa SynthInstrument, SampleInstrument
*/

/*!
    \qmltype SfxPlayer
    \inqmlmodule Clayground.Sound
    \brief Plays an effect recipe (\c .sfx.json) with variation, and renders
    it offline the same way.

    A recipe is a stack of layers - each one voice with a source
    (oscillator, noise or a WAV sample), an envelope, a pitch curve, a
    filter curve, a start offset, a gain and a pan, in Hz, ms, semitones
    and dB - plus a variation spec: a random range per layer field, a
    seed and a round-robin pool size. The plugin README documents the
    format; the parser rejects an unknown field by its name and path.

    \code
    SfxPlayer {
        id: detent
        source: "sfx/wheel-detent.sfx.json"
    }
    // in a handler:
    var seed = detent.play()
    var samples = detent.renderOffline(seed)   // what that play() sounded like
    \endcode

    Every \l play() draws the next variation; a seed always draws the same
    values. \l play() and \l renderOffline() schedule the same notes through
    one code path, so a pre-rendered file of a seed and the live sound of it
    are the same samples. Watches the recipe file and \e{hot-reloads} it; a
    broken save keeps the last good recipe.

    \section1 Properties

    \qmlproperty url SfxPlayer::source
    Path to a \c .sfx.json recipe. Sample files in it are relative to it.

    \qmlproperty bool SfxPlayer::loaded
    True once the recipe parsed and its samples loaded.

    \qmlproperty string SfxPlayer::error
    Last parse or load error, empty when OK. Starts with the path of the
    failing field, e.g. \c{layers[1].envelope: unknown field 'decya'}.

    \qmlproperty string SfxPlayer::name
    The recipe's \c name.

    \qmlproperty int SfxPlayer::layers
    The number of layers in the recipe.

    \qmlproperty int SfxPlayer::pool
    The recipe's \c{variation.pool}: \l play() cycles through this many
    variations; 0 makes every play a new one.

    \qmlproperty int SfxPlayer::lastSeed
    The seed of the last \l play() or \l playSeed(), -1 before the first.

    \qmlproperty real SfxPlayer::volume
    Volume of every layer, 0..1, default 1.

    \qmlproperty real SfxPlayer::pan
    -1 (left) .. +1 (right), added to each layer's own pan. Default 0.

    \qmlproperty string SfxPlayer::bus
    The bus the layers mix into: \c "sfx" (default), \c "music" or
    \c "voice".

    \section1 Methods

    \qmlmethod int SfxPlayer::play(real velocity, real detune)
    Plays the next variation and returns its seed, or -1 when nothing is
    loaded. \a velocity (0..1, default 1) scales every layer. \a detune
    (semitones, default 0) transposes every layer, oscillator and sample:
    the play sounds as the recipe would with every layer's \c detune
    raised by it, so one recipe plays a scale.

    \qmlmethod bool SfxPlayer::playSeed(int seed, real velocity, real detune)
    Plays the variation of \a seed, at \a velocity and transposed by
    \a detune semitones as in \l play(). Returns false when nothing is
    loaded.

    \qmlmethod void SfxPlayer::stop()
    Ends every voice this player started: a ringing layer runs its own
    release from now and is freed, a layer whose offset has not come yet
    does not start. The player stays loaded; the next \l play() sounds
    as before.

    \qmlmethod int SfxPlayer::variationSeed(int index)
    The seed the \a{index}-th \l play() since the recipe loaded uses.

    \qmlmethod list<real> SfxPlayer::renderOffline(int seed, real velocity, real detune)
    What \l playSeed() of \a seed at \a velocity and \a detune (semitones,
    default 0) sends into its bus, as mono
    samples (the (L + R) / 2 downmix, clamped to -1..1) at 44.1 kHz, from
    the first layer's start to the last layer's end, at the player's
    \l volume and \l pan. Renders on an engine of its own and does not
    touch the live sound. The bus volume, echo and reverb are not part of
    it.

    \section1 Signals

    \qmlsignal SfxPlayer::hotReloaded()
    Emitted after the recipe file changed on disk and was loaded again.

    \qmlsignal SfxPlayer::parseError(string message)
    Emitted when the recipe or one of its samples fails to load. On a hot
    reload the previous recipe keeps playing.

    \sa SynthInstrument, SampleInstrument, SongPlayer
*/

/*!
    \qmltype SoundMixer
    \inqmlmodule Clayground.Sound
    \brief The volume, echo and reverb of each mixer bus: sound effects, music and voices.

    A singleton. Every instrument mixes into one of three buses, chosen by
    its \c bus property; the bus volume scales all of it. The volumes
    belong to the process-wide engine, so every QML engine in the process
    sees the same values.

    \qml
    import Clayground.Sound

    Slider { onMoved: SoundMixer.musicVolume = value }
    SynthInstrument { bus: "music" }
    \endqml

    \section1 Properties

    \qmlproperty real SoundMixer::sfxVolume
    Volume of the \c "sfx" bus, 0..1, default 1. Values outside are clamped.

    \qmlproperty real SoundMixer::musicVolume
    Volume of the \c "music" bus, 0..1, default 1.

    \qmlproperty real SoundMixer::voiceVolume
    Volume of the \c "voice" bus, 0..1, default 1.

    \qmlproperty EchoEffect SoundMixer::sfxEcho
    The echo of the \c "sfx" bus. Off by default.

    \qmlproperty EchoEffect SoundMixer::musicEcho
    The echo of the \c "music" bus. Off by default.

    \qmlproperty EchoEffect SoundMixer::voiceEcho
    The echo of the \c "voice" bus. Off by default.

    \qmlproperty ReverbEffect SoundMixer::sfxReverb
    The reverb of the \c "sfx" bus. Off by default.

    \qmlproperty ReverbEffect SoundMixer::musicReverb
    The reverb of the \c "music" bus. Off by default.

    \qmlproperty ReverbEffect SoundMixer::voiceReverb
    The reverb of the \c "voice" bus. Off by default.

    \sa SynthInstrument, SampleInstrument, SongPlayer, EchoEffect, ReverbEffect
*/

/*!
    \qmltype EchoEffect
    \inqmlmodule Clayground.Sound
    \brief The SNES-style echo of one bus: a delay, feedback and an 8-tap FIR filter.

    Not created in QML; every bus has one, reached through \l SoundMixer
    (\c sfxEcho, \c musicEcho, \c voiceEcho). The bus mix is written into
    a delay line; the delayed signal runs through the FIR filter, whose
    output is added to the bus at \l volume and fed back into the line at
    \l feedback. A low-pass FIR darkens every repeat a little more than
    the one before.

    The echo runs before the bus's reverb and before its volume. Turning
    it off drops its tail; turning it on again starts from silence. Its
    values belong to the process-wide engine.

    \qml
    import Clayground.Sound

    Component.onCompleted: {
        SoundMixer.musicEcho.delay = 120
        SoundMixer.musicEcho.feedback = 0.5
        SoundMixer.musicEcho.fir = [0.25, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25]
        SoundMixer.musicEcho.enabled = true
    }
    \endqml

    \section1 Properties

    \qmlproperty bool EchoEffect::enabled
    Whether the echo runs. Default \c false: the bus sounds as without it.

    \qmlproperty real EchoEffect::delay
    Time from a sound to its first repeat in milliseconds, 1..1000,
    default 120.

    \qmlproperty real EchoEffect::feedback
    Share of the filtered echo written back into the delay line, -1..1,
    default 0.5. With the default taps every repeat is this times the
    one before; a negative value flips the sign of every other repeat.

    \qmlproperty real EchoEffect::volume
    Level at which the filtered echo is added to the bus, 0..1, default 0.5.

    \qmlproperty list<real> EchoEffect::fir
    The eight FIR coefficients, -1..1 each, tap 0 the newest sample.
    Default \c {[1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]}, which passes the delayed signal
    unchanged. Fewer than eight values leave the remaining taps 0; more
    are refused with a warning. The taps are consecutive samples at the
    engine's rate. Taps that sum to more than 1 can make the repeats grow;
    the delay line clips, so they cannot run away.

    \sa SoundMixer, ReverbEffect
*/

/*!
    \qmltype ReverbEffect
    \inqmlmodule Clayground.Sound
    \brief The small reverb of one bus.

    Not created in QML; every bus has one, reached through \l SoundMixer
    (\c sfxReverb, \c musicReverb, \c voiceReverb). A Schroeder reverb in
    the Freeverb pattern: four damped feedback combs and two allpasses per
    channel, fed the mono sum of the bus. The right channel's delays are a
    little longer than the left's, so the tail is wide.

    The reverb runs after the bus's echo and before its volume. Turning
    it off drops its tail.

    \section1 Properties

    \qmlproperty bool ReverbEffect::enabled
    Whether the reverb runs. Default \c false.

    \qmlproperty real ReverbEffect::roomSize
    Length of the tail, 0..1, default 0.5.

    \qmlproperty real ReverbEffect::damping
    How fast the high frequencies die in the tail, 0..1, default 0.5.

    \qmlproperty real ReverbEffect::volume
    Level of the reverberated signal added to the bus, 0..1, default 0.3.

    \sa SoundMixer, EchoEffect
*/
